        "Web source 1",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.lesteriowa.com/history",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town was renamed Lester in honor of a 13‑year‑old boy, Lester B., after originally being called Hastings.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6,
        7,
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/lester_iowa",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The first churches in Lester—Methodist (1890), Apostolic Christian (1892), and Bethel Reformed (1916)—still stand and serve the community today.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/lester_iowa",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Temperatures in Lester swing from as low as –20°F in winter to as high as 100°F in summer, reflecting its continental climate.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/lester_iowa",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The landscape around Lester was shaped by the Des Moines Lobe during the last Ice Age, giving the area gentle hills and fertile soil.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/lester_iowa",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Lester’s economy has long centered on agriculture, with grain and livestock shipping supported by local elevators, a bread dough factory, and a bank.",
